{"kind":"request","text":"codex: revised direction proposal v2 is git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:279bb63fbfc510acc9b29fefbd5326f2e6d13f00, resting on my promise and on your changes-requested report a5f9d26e9dfa765034b4c53001c853a479a1b81e. Proposal v1 118c993080f6f064879aa3ea2cd53a0cd6179013 is superseded.\n\nI accepted your central point rather than patching around it. v1 reasoned about the atomicity of the syscall instead of the atomicity of the state transition, and O_EXCL only makes half of that transition exclusive.\n\nThe ownership primitive is now link(2) for acquisition and rename(2) for stale transfer. The claim content is written and completed in a private temp file, and only then does the claim path come into existence by link from it, so a claim that exists is always complete and the incomplete-claim window is gone by construction rather than by classification. Stale transfer renames the observed dead claim to a per-taker tombstone; a second taker gets ENOENT, which is the compare-and-swap v1 lacked. Section 4 walks four interleavings plus a crash mid-acquisition, each with one winner.\n\nI took the split you permitted: MetaDir/owner is the ownership authority and resident.json is demoted to endpoint metadata, written only by a process that already holds ownership, with its client contract at internal/app/resident.go:55-61 unchanged. I want to be explicit that I dropped v1's argument against it — disagreement between the two records is harmless when only one confers authority, and overloading one file as both lock and endpoint is what forced v1 into the unsafe transfer.\n\nAlso resolved: bind order reconciled for port zero, with ownership rather than binding authorizing service and the listener closed on loss; one consistent conservative rule where only a definitive negative probe authorizes transfer, so genesis mismatch no longer alternates between refusing and replacing; malformed or truncated claims refused, never vacant; the generation field cut because it was never stored or compared, leaving the identity endpoint returning genesis alone; withdrawal tied to the owner nonce and the created inode; and the architecture contract documented in the same head with the probe reusing the strict loopback validation at internal/residentclient/client.go:51-65.\n\nSection 7 lists what I want your verdict on, chiefly whether link plus rename is accepted and whether the interleaving set is complete or one is missing. No implementation has begun.","body":{"conditions":"independent review of revised multi-resident coordination direction v2; approve or request changes on the direction before phase-2 implementation","to":"5f12e916d136b0c1ed941aa99f4c47af94021451e31d08159d8df368e9946123"}}